  "markdown_report": "The best first experiment is **terminal-aligned mixed masking within the supplied BERT4Rec**, using one network per category. It targets training–inference alignment while preserving the corrected backbone and evaluation protocol. No improvement is established before validation.\n\n**Insights from the starting point.** First, appended-mask inference predicts from a prefix; interior reconstruction can exploit later interactions, creating a different learning problem. Second, users report both stale interests and broad matches displacing specific choices, motivating supervision on actual next choices. Third, Industrial_and_Scientific and Electronics have wrong-rank rates of 12.25% and 11.27%, identifying weak separation as a measurable concern. Fourth, embedding scores near 0.5 imply mean cosine similarity near zero; they do not justify collapse remedies.\n\n**Insights from related work.** Original [BERT4Rec](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06690) already includes terminal masking, so this proposal refines its sampling rather than inventing that objective. [Recency Sampling](https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643) motivates closer alignment between masking and sequential prediction, without proving gains here. [Sampled Softmax](https://wujcan.github.io/papers/tois-ssm.pdf) highlights objective and temperature sensitivity, supporting a separate controlled objective experiment. [Semantic Fusion](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.18166) motivates contextual content integration, but semantic resemblance cannot establish equipment compatibility.\n\nThe resulting framework separates three directions:\n\n| Direction | Intervention | Diagnostic target | Main uncertainty |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Prediction alignment | Prefix-terminal masking | Current priorities; weak margins | Best sampling proportion |\n| Candidate discrimination | Uniform sampled softmax | Broad matches; wrong ranks | Unobserved false negatives |\n| Content specificity | Joint title/description gate | Fine distinctions | Sparse text and compatibility ambiguity |\n\nThree candidate ideas are assessed below. Scores are hypotheses, not measured outcomes; difficulty 10 means hardest. Each entry gives its principal benefit and limitation.\n\n| Idea | Originality | Potential | User alignment | Model alignment | Difficulty |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mixed prefix-terminal masking | 3: grounded / established | 7: reusable / limited novelty | 7: recent choices / intent latent | 7: aligned separation / gain uncertain | 2: sampler change / leakage checks |\n| Uniform sampled softmax | 3: established / little novelty | 7: scalable / tuning sensitive | 5: discrimination / weak specificity | 8: direct separation / false negatives | 4: shared candidates / exclusions |\n| Gated text–ID embeddings | 5: useful combination / precedented | 7: richer inputs / sparse metadata | 8: finer distinctions / no ownership evidence | 6: richer features / benefit uncertain | 6: joint encoder / predictor changes |\n\nSelect mixed prefix-terminal masking at 0% research progress. Use terminal examples for half the training examples and retain corrected interior masking for the remainder. Sample a training prefix, remove its target and every later interaction, then append the reserved mask. Preserve the baseline loss initially to isolate sampling.\n\nThis explicitly targets two user failures: stale historical emphasis and broad relevance displacing specific next choices. It also targets weak positive–negative separation, without claiming to infer compatibility. Compare against the supplied baseline using macro validation HR@10; margin diagnostics remain secondary. Unknown-item skips remain unassessable feedback.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Replace half of corrected BERT4Rec training examples with randomly sampled prefix-terminal masked examples, retaining one shared bidirectional Transformer and the baseline loss. The terminal target and every subsequent interaction are excluded from that example's input.",
    "motivation": "Terminal examples match appended-mask inference and emphasize observed next choices. This addresses stale-interest emphasis and insufficient distinction between broadly relevant items and specific next choices, while testing whether the weaker margin tails in Industrial_and_Scientific and Electronics improve. These are hypotheses; neither compatibility nor present intent is directly observed.",
    "implementation_notes": "Keep the supplied training, data routing, checkpoint selection and predictor writer. Change only family-specific example sampling initially; no architecture or inference change is required. Apply the example-type mixture per example, with probability 0.5 for terminal masking. Retain the corrected baseline's interior-mask branch and loss unchanged. Use only each user's training history. Seed preprocessing with 0 and training with 42; keep at most 200 epochs and exactly 200 batches per epoch. Preserve DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'] and trial-local artifacts. Train and load exactly one checkpoint per category. Preserve ModelView.item_emb, log2feats(sequence), predict_score(features, items), user_train, meta_data and review_data. DiagnosisProbe must score only supervised masked positions; it must not score visible targets or interpret unavailable categories. Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), with measured metrics and definitions. The helper below returns an example, its aligned positive labels, and its supervision mask; connect these to the supplied sample_batch and existing loss rather than replacing the host pipeline. The available prompt contains no baseline source, so this is an integration helper, not a standalone train.py replacement.",
    "pseudocode": "import numpy as np\n\n\ndef terminal_masked_example(history, maxlen, mask_id, rng):\n    \"\"\"history contains only real training-item IDs; rng is seeded by caller.\"\"\"\n    if maxlen < 2 or len(history) < 2:\n        raise ValueError('Need room for context and a mask, and two interactions')\n    # t is the target index. Neither history[t] nor any later item is input.\n    t = int(rng.integers(1, len(history)))\n    context = np.asarray(history[max(0, t - maxlen + 1):t], dtype=np.int64)\n    sequence = np.zeros(maxlen, dtype=np.int64)\n    positive = np.zeros(maxlen, dtype=np.int64)\n    sequence[-len(context)-1:-1] = context\n    sequence[-1] = mask_id\n    positive[-1] = int(history[t])\n    supervised = positive != 0\n    return sequence, positive, supervised\n\n\n# In the supplied family-specific sample_batch:\n# if rng.random() < 0.5 and len(training_history) >= 2:\n#     sequence, positive, supervised = terminal_masked_example(\n#         training_history, maxlen, reserved_mask_id, rng)\n# else:\n#     sequence, positive, supervised = corrected_interior_mask_example(...)\n# Generate negatives using the supplied training-only sampler.\n# Apply the existing masked-item loss only where supervised is True.\n# Retain (None, sequence, positive, negative), each tensor shaped B x L.\n# In inference, retain the supplied appended-mask construction.\n# For left-padded examples above the readout is features[:, -1], never count-1.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 3,
      "positive": "A controlled prefix-sampling refinement directly connects the observed failure patterns to inference-aligned supervision.",
      "negative": "Terminal masking is already part of original BERT4Rec; the contribution is experimental design and sampling emphasis."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "A low-cost experiment can establish whether objective alignment deserves further work before adding content or mining.",
      "negative": "It cannot resolve missing compatibility evidence, and stronger training margins may not improve validation HR@10."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 2,
      "positive": "The change is confined primarily to example construction and preserves architecture and checkpoint formats.",
      "negative": "Target exclusion, truncation, supervised-position diagnostics and left-padding readout require careful integration."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "BERT4Rec: Sequential Recommendation with Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformer",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06690",
      "contributions": [
        "Introduces bidirectional masked-item sequential recommendation.",
        "Includes terminal masking and appended-mask next-item inference."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish the best prefix-terminal sampling proportion for this benchmark."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recency Sampling",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643",
      "contributions": [
        "Motivates sampling objectives better aligned with sequential recommendation and recency."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not validate the proposed mixture on the four supplied datasets under the pinned evaluator."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Sampled Softmax for Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://wujcan.github.io/papers/tois-ssm.pdf",
      "contributions": [
        "Studies efficient shared negative sampling and the influence of temperature."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Its findings do not establish improvements for this corrected BERT4Rec setup.",
        "Unobserved sampled items may be false negatives."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Sequence-level Semantic Representation Fusion",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.18166",
      "contributions": [
        "Motivates contextual integration of semantic and item-ID representations."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish that a randomly initialized lightweight text encoder improves this benchmark.",
        "Semantic similarity alone cannot establish compatibility or equipment ownership."
      ]
    }
  ]
